174
00:13:25,793 --> 00:13:29,270
An image tag is a name, and names
differ in whether they move.

175
00:13:29,610 --> 00:13:35,680
Quick quiz. Three image tags: main,
latest, and sha- plus the short commit ID.

176
00:13:35,720 --> 00:13:39,676
Which one would you write
into a deployment record?

177
00:13:39,716 --> 00:13:43,514
A, main. B, latest. C, the
sha- tag. Your answer?

178
00:13:48,354 --> 00:13:53,635
C, the one that names a commit. main moves
on every push to main, and latest moves

179
00:13:53,675 --> 00:13:58,890
too. If you said latest, that's a common
habit, and the diagram shows why to drop

180
00:13:58,930 --> 00:13:59,085
it.

181
00:13:59,425 --> 00:14:03,307
Where this model breaks: "pinned"
doesn't mean "safe" or "current".

182
00:14:03,347 --> 00:14:08,167
A pin to a commit with a known problem stays
on that commit until someone moves it.

183
00:14:08,207 --> 00:14:12,792
That's why the pin file says pins go stale
on purpose, and why something has to

184
00:14:12,832 --> 00:14:13,728
propose updates.

185
00:14:15,568 --> 00:14:19,445
Four names on one image. Ask of
each one: does this name move?

186
00:14:19,785 --> 00:14:25,332
The sha- tag names one commit, and doesn't
move. main names a branch tip, and moves

187
00:14:25,372 --> 00:14:29,573
on every push to main. 0.2.0 names
a version, from the Git tag.

188
00:14:29,613 --> 00:14:33,007
latest comes along with a
semver tag, and it moves.

189
00:14:33,047 --> 00:14:37,921
On a pull request, pr- and the number
is computed, and nothing is pushed.

